        "original_id": "f8a95cbd-7c7f-5286-9344-7e7270ae4b90",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Alexander Kanoldt\n- Movements/Genres: Magic realism, New Objectivity\n- Topics: Narrative fiction, art, fabulism\n\nFacts:\n- Alexander Kanoldt was born on 29 September 1881 and died on 24 January 1939.\n- Alexander Kanoldt was a German magic realist painter and one of the artists of the New Objectivity.\n- Magic realism, also called magical realism or marvelous realism, is a genre of narrative fiction and more broadly of art including literature, painting, film, and theatre.\n- Magic realism presents a primarily realistic view of the world while also adding or revealing magical elements.\n- Magic realism is sometimes called fabulism in reference to the conventions of fables, myths, and allegory.\n- The term magical realism often refers specifically to fiction and literature in which magic or the supernatural appears in an otherwise real-world or mundane setting.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "c2d96530-fb3d-5a3c-b7d7-a6ff168ccfdf",
        "text": "Page: Alexander Kanoldt. Sentence: Alexander Kanoldt (29 September 1881 – 24 January 1939) was a German magic realist painter and one of the artists of the New Objectivity.\nPage: Magic realism. Sentence: Magical realism, magic realism, or marvelous realism is a genre of narrative fiction and, more broadly, art (literature, painting, film, theatre, etc.) that, while encompassing a range of subtly different concepts, expresses a primarily realistic view of the real world while also adding or revealing magical elements.\nPage: Magic realism. Sentence: It is sometimes called fabulism, in reference to the conventions of fables, myths, and allegory.\nPage: Magic realism. Sentence: \"Magical realism\", perhaps the most common term, often refers to fiction and literature in particular, with magic or the supernatural presented in an otherwise real-world or mundane setting.",
